-- title:
--   (3.7) — $v(\mu)=\sup_{h\in\mathcal H}Q(\mu,h)$
-- statement:
--   Assume Outstanding Assumption (A). For every $\mu\in\mathcal P(\mathcal S)$:
--
--   1. the values $\{v^\pi(\mu)\}_{\pi\in\Pi}$ are bounded above;
--   2. for every $h\in\mathcal H$, the values $\{Q^\pi(\mu,h)\}_{\pi\in\Pi_1}$ are bounded above;
--   3. the values $\{Q(\mu,h)\}_{h\in\mathcal H}$ are bounded above;
--   4. the value function is the supremum of the IQ function over local policies,
--   $$v(\mu)=\sup_{h\in\mathcal H}Q(\mu,h).$$
--
--   This is the lifted analogue of $v(s)=\sup_aQ(s,a)$ for Markov decision processes, and it turns $\sup_{h'}Q(\Phi(\mu,h),h')$ in the Bellman equation into the value function.
--
--   **Formalization Note** All suprema are real suprema; clauses 1–3 show they are genuine suprema of bounded families, not the default value $0$ of an unbounded one.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--
--   - generalization: v(µ) = sup_{h∈H} Q(µ,h) → the same identity together with: {v^π(µ)}_π, {Q^π(µ,h)}_π for each h, and {Q(µ,h)}_h are bounded above; reason: the paper treats these suprema as finite reals (the ε-optimal π^ε of (3.10) and the equality of reals in (3.7)); dropping the boundedness clauses gives the printed statement
--   - encoding: Π₁ = controls {π_t}_{t≥1} starting at time 1 → an ℕ-indexed Control whose t-th kernel plays at time t + 1 (Control.cons, Control.shift); reason: the model is time-homogeneous; the re-indexing is a bijection and the paper itself uses sup_{π∈Π₁} v^π = v (p.10)
--   - encoding: E^π[Σ_{t=0}^∞ γ^t r(s_t, µ_t, a_t, ν_t)] → Bochner integral of ω ↦ ∑' t, γ^t r_t(ω) under the canonical path law; reason: under (A) the series converges absolutely almost surely and is integrable (Lemma 2.2), so ∑' is the sum of the series and the integral is the expectation
--   - encoding: Outstanding Assumption (A): for any s₀ = ξ ∼ µ, sup_{π∈Π} E^π[Σ_t γ^t |r(s_t, µ_t, a_t, ν_t)|] < ∞ → for every µ, the supremum over controls of the lintegral of Σ_t γ^t |r_t| under the canonical path law is < ⊤; reason: a lintegral of a nonnegative series is the expectation without any integrability side condition; the expectation is the same for every realization of (2.7) (Lemmas 2.1–2.2), and the bound is per µ, as printed
--   - reading: (2.7) gives the one-step laws of s_{t+1}, a_t and r_t and leaves the joint conditional law of (r_t, s_{t+1}) given (s_t, a_t) open → the canonical path law draws r_t and s_{t+1} conditionally independently given (s_t, a_t); reason: every expectation in the paper depends only on the laws of (s_t, a_t, r_t), which Lemmas 2.1–2.2 show are the same for every realization of (2.7)
--   - generalization: S, A compact separable metric spaces with their Borel σ-fields; P(X) with W₁ and its Borel σ-field (pp.3–4) → S, A arbitrary measurable spaces; P(X) = ProbabilityMeasure X with the Giry σ-algebra; reason: no metric or compactness is used; the paper identifies the Borel σ-field of P(X) with the σ-field generated by µ ↦ µ(C) (p.3), which is the Giry σ-algebra, so the compact Borel case is an instance
--   - encoding: H = {h : S → P(A) measurable such that Outstanding Assumption (A) holds} (2.9) → H = all Markov kernels S → A (LocalPolicy); reason: (A) is a standing assumption over all of Π and every measurable h is the constant control π_t ≡ h ∈ Π, so the side condition excludes nothing
-- source:
--   Gu, Guo, Wei, Xu, Dynamic Programming Principles for Mean-Field Controls with Learning, arXiv:1911.07314v6, proof of Theorem 3.1, (3.7)–(3.12), pp.9–10

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MFCLearn_Model
import Definitions.Def_MFCLearn_Lifted
import Definitions.Def_MFCLearn_Process

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace MFCLearn

/-- **(3.7): the value function is the supremum of the IQ function.** Gu, Guo, Wei, Xu, *Dynamic
Programming Principles for Mean-Field Controls with Learning*, arXiv:1911.07314v6, proof of
Theorem 3.1, (3.7), p.9: "v(μ) = sup_{h∈H} Q(μ, h), for any μ ∈ P(S)". Under Outstanding Assumption
(A), for every `μ`: the families `{v^π(μ)}_{π∈Π}`, `{Q^π(μ, h)}_{π∈Π₁}` (each `h`) and `{Q(μ, h)}_{h∈H}`
are bounded above, and `v(μ) = sup_{h∈H} Q(μ, h)`.

**Formalization Note** All suprema are real `⨆`; the boundedness clauses show they are genuine
suprema, not the junk value `0` of an unbounded family. -/
theorem eq_3_7 {S A : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] [MeasurableSpace A]
    (m : MFCModel S A) (hA : m.OutstandingA) (μ : ProbabilityMeasure S) :
    BddAbove (Set.range fun π : Control S A => m.vpi π μ) ∧
      (∀ h : LocalPolicy S A, BddAbove (Set.range fun π : Control S A => m.Qpi π μ h)) ∧
      BddAbove (Set.range fun h : LocalPolicy S A => m.Q μ h) ∧
      m.v μ = ⨆ h : LocalPolicy S A, m.Q μ h := by sorry

end MFCLearn
